:name: MIMXRT1189-EVK (CM33) — Tier A prebuilt .bin
:description: Stock NXP SDK PREBUILT cm33 .bin images from SDK_26_06_00_MIMXRT1180-EVK.zip

using sysbus
include @/home/kyle/Documents/GitHub/rt1180renode/scripts/load_peripherals.resc

mach create "rt1180"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @/home/kyle/Documents/GitHub/rt1180renode/platforms/mimxrt1189_m1.repl


# ⭐ BLANKET TrustZone-M SECURE MIRROR.
# The whole NS peripheral window 0x4000_0000..0x4FFF_FFFF is mirrored at
# 0x5000_0000 in ONE statement, instead of giving each peripheral its own
# "+0x1000_0000" registration.
#
# WHY: per-peripheral aliasing is structurally prone to silently omitting the
# secure alias of any block the test corpus never touches secure-side. That is
# not hypothetical -- it happened here: S3MU was the ONE block without its alias,
# because every NXP SDK example runs non-secure, and only Zephyr (which runs the
# cm33 SECURE) exposed it by polling S3MU TSR at 0x5754_0124.
# A blanket mirror is immune by construction.
# Architecture noted by @rt1180emulator, whose QEMU SoC does the same thing with
# one memory_region_init_alias(0x40000000, size 0x10000000) mapped at 0x50000000.
sysbus Redirect 0x50000000 0x40000000 0x10000000

showAnalyzer lpuart1
cpu PerformanceInMips 240

# ⚠ THE LOAD BASE IS NOT CONSTANT. A flat .bin carries no load address, and the
# SDK cm33 images do NOT all link to the base of CODE_TCM: the three
# usb_device_dfu images link at 0x0FFF0000 (reserving the low 64 KiB of CODE_TCM
# as the DFU update slot) while hello_world/led_blinky/sai/bubble/multicore_trigger
# link at 0x0FFE0000. MEASURED from each image's own reset vector (file word[1]):
#     0x0FFE047D -> base 0x0FFE0000   (5 images)
#     0x0FFF4F91 -> base 0x0FFF0000   (3 usb images)
# So $loadbase is DERIVED per-image as (reset_pc & 0xFFFF0000), which is exactly
# what the QEMU oracle does (hw/arm/imxrt1180_evk.c, the !is_elf branch).
# Hardcoding 0x0FFE0000 put the USB images' reset PC 64 KiB past the end of the
# loaded blob -> 'Trying to execute code outside RAM or ROM at 0x10000000'.
# That was a HARNESS error of mine, not a Renode gap.
#
# Tier A images are raw .bin, not ELF. They link to ITCM and carry a standard
# ARMv8-M vector table at offset 0 (SP at +0, reset vector at +4) -- DERIVED from
# the images themselves: SP=0x20020000 (DTCM top = 0x20000000 + 0x20000) and
# PC=0x0FFE047D (ITCM, thumb bit set). So: load at ITCM base and point VTOR there;
# Renode's CortexM then fetches SP/PC from the table exactly as the hardware does.
macro reset
"""
    sysbus LoadBinary $bin $loadbase
    cpu VectorTableOffset $loadbase
"""
runMacro $reset
